course: world
lesson: 79
title: Senses of animals: how they see, hear and smell
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every animal's senses fit the way it lives: a hunter needs to find its food, and the hunted need to notice danger in time.
explain: Eyes at the front of the head, like an owl's or a cat's, both look at the same thing and judge distance well for catching prey; eyes at the sides, like a rabbit's or a horse's, see almost all the way around to spot enemies.
explain: Some animals sense things we cannot: bees see ultraviolet patterns on flowers, bats hear very high sounds, and dogs smell scents thousands of times weaker than we can.
explain: Bats and dolphins use echoes: they make a sound, listen for it to bounce back, and work out where things are, even in complete darkness or murky water.
explain: A shark can smell a tiny trace of blood in the water from far away, and it also feels the weak electricity made by the muscles of fish hiding in the sand.
fact: Animals with eyes on the front of the head judge distance well.
fact: Animals with eyes on the sides of the head can see almost all around them.
fact: Most hunters have eyes at the front, and most hunted animals have eyes at the sides.
fact: Bees can see ultraviolet light, which people cannot see; many flowers have ultraviolet patterns that guide bees to the nectar.
fact: Bats find insects in the dark by making high sounds and listening to the echoes.
fact: Dolphins also find food by using echoes.
fact: A dog's sense of smell is many thousands of times better than a person's.
fact: Snakes smell by flicking their tongue to pick up scents from the air.
fact: Cats see much better than people in dim light, but they cannot see in total darkness.
fact: Some snakes, such as rattlesnakes, can sense the heat given off by warm animals.
fact: Sharks can sense the weak electricity made by living animals.
fact: Owls hear so well that they can catch a mouse they cannot see.
exercise: Where are the eyes of most hunting animals? | At the front of the head | At the sides of the head | On top of the head | At the back of the head
exercise: Why does a rabbit have eyes on the sides of its head? | To see danger coming from almost any direction | To judge distance well when hunting other animals | To see better at night | To see ultraviolet colours
exercise: How do bats find insects in the dark? | They make sounds and listen for the echoes | They see them easily with their very large eyes | They smell them from far away in the night air | They feel the wind from the insects' tiny wings
exercise: What can bees see that people cannot? | Ultraviolet patterns on flowers | Sounds coming from the hive | Heat from the sun at night | The smell of nectar as a colour
exercise: What does a snake use its flicking tongue for? | To pick up smells from the air | To taste the ground for water | To scare enemies with a hiss | To hear sounds through the air
exercise: How much better is a dog's sense of smell than ours? | Many thousands of times better | About two times better | It is about the same as ours | It is a little worse than ours
exercise: Which is true about cats' eyes? | They see well in dim light but not in total darkness | They see perfectly in total darkness | They see worse than people at night | They glow because they make their own light
exercise: What extra sense helps a rattlesnake hunt at night? | It senses the heat of warm animals | It hears the little mouse's heartbeat | It sees the ultraviolet light of the mouse | It uses echoes of its hisses like a bat
exercise: How can a shark find a fish hidden in the sand? | It senses the electricity of the fish's muscles | It sees right through the sand with its sharp eyes | It hears the fish breathing air | It tastes the sand from far away
exercise: Which other animal uses echoes to find food? | The dolphin | The cow | The tortoise | The butterfly
exercise: How can an owl catch a mouse under the snow? | By hearing it very precisely | By smelling it through the snow | By seeing through the snow | By feeling the mouse's heat with its feet
exercise: Why do eyes at the front help a hunter? | Both eyes see the same thing, so it judges distance well | They let it see behind its own back | They let it see in total darkness | They make the hunter look bigger
exercise: Why do many flowers have ultraviolet patterns? | To guide bees to the nectar | To keep the flower warm | To hide the flower from bees | To scare away birds
exercise: Why are dogs used to search for lost people? | They can follow a person's smell | They see much further than people | They can hear people's thoughts | They use echoes to find people
test: A deer and a wolf meet in a forest. Which one can see more of the forest around it without turning its head? | The deer, with eyes at the sides | The wolf, with eyes at the front | Both see exactly the same | Neither can see without turning
test: You find a skull of an unknown animal with large eyes facing forward. What was it most likely? | A hunter | A grass-eater that hides from hunters | A blind cave animal | An animal that lived only underground
test: Why could a bat still catch moths in a completely dark cave? | It listens for the echoes of its own sounds | It sees perfectly well even in total darkness | Moths glow in the dark, so it can spot them easily | It smells the dust on the moths' wings from far off
test: A garden has a flower that looks plain white to you. Why might bees still find it very easy to spot? | It may have ultraviolet markings bees can see | Bees cannot see white at all, so they never visit | Bees only visit plain flowers | White flowers have no nectar
test: What would happen if a rattlesnake lost its heat sense? | It would find it harder to catch prey at night | It could no longer smell anything around it at all | It would become completely blind in bright daylight | It would hunt even better than before, by day and night
test: Which is true? | Cats need at least a little light to see | Cats see perfectly well in total darkness | Cats are blind in daylight | Cats see only black and white shapes
test: A diver is bleeding a little in the sea. Why should they leave the water quickly? | Sharks can smell blood from far away | Sharks can see the colour red from very far | Blood makes the water around it too cold | Blood attracts the dolphins' clicking echoes
test: Why does a dolphin hunting in muddy water not need clear eyesight? | It finds fish by their echoes | It smells fish through its skin | It catches only fish that glow | It waits for fish to touch it
test: A puppy keeps sniffing the ground on a walk. What is it doing? | Reading the smells left by other animals | Looking for warm spots to sleep | Listening to worms under the ground | Testing the ground for ultraviolet light
test: how does an owl get a mouse it cant even see | It hears so well it can catch the mouse by sound | It smells the mouse from far away | It sees in total darkness | It feels the mouse's heat with the tip of its beak
test: why's a rabbit got eyes on the sides of its head | So it can see almost all around for danger | So it can judge distance well for hunting | So it can see ultraviolet light | So it can see better in total darkness
test: Ava says bats are blind and just bump around. true? | No, bats find insects in the dark using echoes of their sounds | Yes, bats fly around with no way of finding food | Yes, but only the bats that eat fruit | No, bats see perfectly in total darkness
test: my sister reckons cats can see in total darkness. right? | No, cats see well in dim light but not in total darkness | Yes, cats see just as well with no light at all | Yes, their eyes make their own light | No, cats see worse than people in dim light
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the advantage of eyes positioned on the sides of the head? | They permit vision almost all around | They permit the accurate judging of distance | They permit vision in total darkness | They permit vision of ultraviolet light
exam: Identify the means by which bats locate insects in the dark. | By making high sounds and listening to the echoes | By detecting the warmth of the insects | By smelling the insects from afar | By seeing in total darkness
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the vision of cats. | They see much better than people in dim light, but not in total darkness | They see perfectly in total darkness | They see less well than people in dim light | They see by means of echoes in the dark
exam: Ascertain the capacity which enables sharks to detect living animals. | They sense the weak electricity made by living animals | They sense the ultraviolet light of living animals | They sense the magnetism of living animals | They sense the heat of animals with their tongue
